<h2>The statutory architecture</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 135.</strong> Theft of electricity — dishonest tapping, tampering with meters, damaging equipment or using unauthorised devices — punishable with imprisonment which may extend to three years or fine or both, with minimum fines pegged to the connected load and multiples of the financial gain.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 151B.</strong> Offences under Sections 135 to 140 and 150 are cognizable and non-bailable, displacing the classification the general Code would otherwise supply.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Sections 153&ndash;154.</strong> Special Courts, constituted for speedy trial of these offences, try the cases; the ordinary magistracy does not.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Section 152.</strong> Compounding: payment at prescribed rates closes the criminal case for that theft, with the statute discouraging repeat recourse.</div>
