<h2>How bail is actually decided</h2>
<p>Although non-bailable, electricity theft sits at the lighter end of the non-bailable spectrum: a maximum of three years, a documentary and technical case, and an identifiable monetary dimension. Bail hearings therefore revolve around a familiar cluster of factors, with two distinctive features.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The inspection record is the case.</strong> The seizure memo, the meter or artificial means found, the load report and the laboratory test of the meter largely fix the evidence at the outset — which blunts the usual apprehension of evidence-tampering and favours bail once the documentation is complete.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The quantum matters.</strong> The assessed units and the theft bill give the court a concrete measure of gravity; a small domestic assessment and a large industrial direct-theft case are approached very differently.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conduct towards the licensee counts.</strong> Deposit of the assessed amount, or part of it, and cooperation with the inspection are weighed in favour of the accused; obstruction of the raiding team, which is separately punishable, weighs the other way.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Anticipatory bail is available.</strong> There is no statutory bar on pre-arrest bail under Section 482 BNSS, and in genuinely disputed cases — contested meter readings, tenancy disputes over who consumed the power — interim protection with a direction to join the investigation is a realistic outcome.</div>
</div>

<h2>Two tracks, one strategy</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Track</th><th>Nature</th><th>Practical use</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Criminal: FIR under Section 135, trial before the Special Court</td><td>Penal; ends in acquittal, conviction or compounding under Section 152</td><td>Contest where the inspection is procedurally flawed or consumption is genuinely attributable elsewhere; compound where exposure is clear.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Civil: assessment and theft billing (Section 126 for unauthorised use; billing for theft cases)</td><td>Monetary; appealable through the statutory channel</td><td>Challenge the computation — load, period, tariff multiplier — independently of guilt; settlement supports the bail and compounding effort.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The interplay is where cases are won or sensibly closed. A client who disputes the theft should preserve the premises as inspected, obtain the inspection documents immediately, and attack the computation in the civil channel while defending the prosecution. A client whose exposure is real is usually better served by early compounding: it is a statutory discharge, not a favour, and it avoids a conviction that carries consequences for licences, employment and future tariffs.</p>

<h2>Checklist on receiving an inspection report or FIR</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Collect the paperwork on day one: inspection report, seizure memo, panchnama, photographs or videography of the premises, and the speaking order or bill that follows.</p>
  <p>Verify who is actually liable — the registered consumer, the occupier, or a tenant — because prosecutions frequently name the owner for a tenant&rsquo;s consumption, and that defence is best raised early and documented.</p>
  <p>Assess the compounding arithmetic before the first bail hearing: appearing with a concrete settlement proposal often converts a contested hearing into a short one.</p>
  <p>Remember the Special Court: applications filed in the wrong forum lose days, and in arrest cases days matter.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the statutory scheme in general terms for information. Rates of compounding and billing formulas are prescribed by regulations and differ by class of consumer; the figures in any individual case must be checked against the current instruments.</div>
